1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:04,500
Okay, welcome to the chapter on breast pathology.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,020
The very first point that I'd like to make is actually a point from embryology, and that

3
00:00:08,020 --> 00:00:11,060
is that the breast is a modified skin gland.

4
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:15,780
So what happens is you get an involution of the skin, and that eventually results in

5
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:19,820
the breast, so that, again, the breast is derived from the skin.

6
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:24,140
One important thing to know about embryology of the breast, which is particularly high-yield,

7
00:00:24,140 --> 00:00:28,820
is that the breast actually develops from something called the milk line, and the milk

8
00:00:28,820 --> 00:00:32,660
line actually runs all the way from the axilla down to the vulva.

9
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:37,000
So if you took a point at the axilla and then took another point at the vulva and just sort

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,580
of made a straight line, breast tissue can develop anywhere along that line, and that line is

11
00:00:41,580 --> 00:00:42,940
called the milk line.

12
00:00:42,940 --> 00:00:47,120
And so, for example, patients can have extra nipples, and the nipples could be anywhere along

13
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:48,240
that line.

14
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:51,680
Patients can actually have extra breast tissue, and that breast tissue can be anywhere along

15
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:53,220
that line.

16
00:00:53,220 --> 00:00:54,220
And so can pathology.

17
00:00:54,220 --> 00:00:57,960
So for example, they can have breast cancer anywhere along that line as well.

18
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:02,160
It's relatively rare for these types of disorders to occur, but of course, what's rare shows

19
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:05,720
up on exams, so it's important to be aware of it.

20
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:10,960
Now the functional unit of the breast, histologically, is called the terminal duct lobular unit.

21
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:15,040
And the idea here is that you've got this duct, and this duct feeds into something called a

22
00:01:15,040 --> 00:01:19,120
lobule, and the lobule contains these little glandular spaces.

23
00:01:19,120 --> 00:01:22,800
And these little glandular spaces, they are the lobules that make milk.

24
00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:26,240
So this is the lobule, and this is the duct.

25
00:01:26,240 --> 00:01:30,180
It's the job of the lobule to make milk, and then it's the job of the duct to drain that

26
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:31,980
milk out to the nipple.

27
00:01:31,980 --> 00:01:36,980
And this functional unit here, this entire thing, this is the terminal duct, the end of the duct,

28
00:01:36,980 --> 00:01:38,080
and this is the lobule.

29
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:42,220
So that's called the TDLU, or the terminal duct lobular unit.

30
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:44,360
Here's a picture of that histologically.

31
00:01:44,360 --> 00:01:48,380
So you can see that this would be, for example, the terminal duct cut on end, and then you've

32
00:01:48,380 --> 00:01:53,820
got all these little lobular spaces with this entire thing representing the lobule, and

33
00:01:53,820 --> 00:01:59,260
then all these little glandular spaces which would be producing the milk in the proper physiologic

34
00:01:59,260 --> 00:02:00,720
context.

35
00:02:00,720 --> 00:02:03,320
So this is, that's the normal histology.

36
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:07,520
Now one super high yield point to be aware of is that all the ducts in the breast and all

37
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:12,760
the lobules in the breast, so the entire, basically, breast epithelium is actually two layers

38
00:02:12,760 --> 00:02:14,320
of epithelium.

39
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:18,360
Such that, if you were to look at the epithelium of the breast, it would look like the following.

40
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:22,140
Let's take a duct, for example, so this is a duct, and one of the important things is

41
00:02:22,140 --> 00:02:27,100
that the duct would obviously be lined by a layer of epithelial cells, and these epithelial

42
00:02:27,100 --> 00:02:29,940
cells are also called luminal cells.

43
00:02:29,940 --> 00:02:34,600
These luminal cells in the duct, they play a role to protect the duct, so they're the protective

44
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:35,640
layer of the duct.

45
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:38,260
In the lobule, they play the role of making the milk.

46
00:02:38,260 --> 00:02:43,000
Now underneath these luminal cells will always be another set of cells called myoepithelial

47
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:44,660
cells.

48
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:48,760
They're called myo because they have contractile function, and they're called epithelial because

49
00:02:48,760 --> 00:02:54,440
they help to produce the epithelial lining of this particular space.

50
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:59,040
And so myoepithelial cells, they contain contractile function, and it's their contractile function

51
00:02:59,040 --> 00:03:02,260
that helps to squeeze the duct to push the milk forward.

52
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:03,920
But again, super high yield.

53
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:10,520
The ducts and lobules each are lined by two layers of epithelium, an inner luminal layer,

54
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:13,420
and an outer myoepithelial layer.

55
00:03:13,420 --> 00:03:16,920
Going one step further, we should remind ourselves that the breast tissue is hormone sensitive.

56
00:03:16,920 --> 00:03:22,360
So when you look at breast epithelium, it contains both estrogen and progesterone receptors, and

57
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:26,300
those receptors are necessary because it's estrogen and progesterone that are going to

58
00:03:26,300 --> 00:03:28,480
drive the development of the breast.

59
00:03:28,480 --> 00:03:33,860
For example, when you look at a boy or a girl, they both have minimal breast tissue underneath

60
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:35,580
the subareolar region.

61
00:03:35,580 --> 00:03:42,580
However, once a girl goes through puberty and begins to produce estrogen and progesterone,

62
00:03:42,580 --> 00:03:46,100
then there's going to be significant development of the breast after menarche.

63
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:50,280
The lobules are going to begin to develop, and the breast will begin to expand.

64
00:03:50,280 --> 00:03:54,380
And it's important to note anatomically in this particular context that the highest density

65
00:03:54,380 --> 00:03:59,720
of breast tissue in a female actually ends up being in the upper outer quadrant of the breast.

66
00:03:59,720 --> 00:04:03,280
So in the upper outer quadrant of the breast, anatomically, is the highest density of breast

67
00:04:03,280 --> 00:04:04,280
tissue.

68
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:08,420
It's a high-yield principle, as you'll see moving forward.

69
00:04:08,420 --> 00:04:13,240
Another important consequence of the breast being hormone sensitive is that there's breast

70
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:15,800
tenderness during the menstrual cycle.

71
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:19,320
So that's another sign of the breast being hormone sensitive.

72
00:04:19,320 --> 00:04:22,180
Another consequence is that there's hyperplasia during pregnancy.

73
00:04:22,180 --> 00:04:26,060
Remember that during pregnancy, there's a lot of estrogen and progesterone produced.

74
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:30,220
You can go back to normal physiology and remind yourself that the corpus luteum produces

75
00:04:30,220 --> 00:04:33,980
a ton of estrogen and progesterone in the very first about 10 weeks of pregnancy.

76
00:04:33,980 --> 00:04:38,120
And then after that, the placenta produces progesterone.

77
00:04:38,120 --> 00:04:40,680
And the fetal placental unit produces estrogen.

78
00:04:40,680 --> 00:04:44,220
But the point that I'm trying to make here is that the estrogen and progesterone during

79
00:04:44,220 --> 00:04:48,120
pregnancy, they will drive development of the breast so that the breast becomes bigger.

80
00:04:48,120 --> 00:04:51,700
And of course, we want it to become bigger because it's eventually going to produce the

81
00:04:51,700 --> 00:04:54,120
milk necessary for the baby.

82
00:04:54,120 --> 00:04:58,460
And then finally, when a woman undergoes menopause and the amount of estrogen and progesterone

83
00:04:58,460 --> 00:05:00,820
decrease, then the breast atrophies.

84
00:05:00,820 --> 00:05:06,480
So all of these are basically signs of hormonal sensitivity of breast tissue.

85
00:05:06,480 --> 00:05:10,520
The very first disorder that we want to talk about is called galacteria.

86
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:12,260
Galacteria refers to the production of milk.

87
00:05:12,260 --> 00:05:14,740
However, it is outside of lactation, all right?

88
00:05:14,740 --> 00:05:17,500
So if a woman is breastfeeding, she doesn't have galacteria.

89
00:05:17,500 --> 00:05:22,440
But if she is producing milk and she's not feeding, i.e., she's not recently pregnant,

90
00:05:22,580 --> 00:05:24,600
et cetera, then we call that galacteria.

91
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:28,700
Now, the first thing I'd like to remind you of is the production of milk is not a symptom

92
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:29,640
of breast cancer.

93
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:33,380
So you're not going to find galacteria in the context of breast cancer.

94
00:05:33,860 --> 00:05:34,140
All right?

95
00:05:34,200 --> 00:05:36,460
So galacteria is not related to breast cancer.

96
00:05:36,840 --> 00:05:38,800
Now, what are some things that can cause galacteria?

97
00:05:38,800 --> 00:05:43,780
Well, there's one important physiologic cause, and then there are some common pathologic

98
00:05:43,780 --> 00:05:44,200
causes.

99
00:05:44,620 --> 00:05:46,760
A physiologic cause could be nipple stimulation.

100
00:05:47,280 --> 00:05:51,140
So if there's excessive nipple stimulation, that can actually result in overproduction

101
00:05:51,140 --> 00:05:52,100
of milk by the breast.

102
00:05:52,560 --> 00:05:56,680
In fact, what's particularly interesting is that if a woman adopts a baby and would like

103
00:05:56,680 --> 00:06:01,260
to try to breastfeed that baby, there are techniques where she can undergo nipple stimulation, and

104
00:06:01,260 --> 00:06:03,200
that would result in the production of milk.

105
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:07,260
So galacteria could be seen with excessive nipple stimulation.

106
00:06:07,260 --> 00:06:12,460
Also, a prolactinoma of the anterior pituitary, which would produce prolactin, which would

107
00:06:12,460 --> 00:06:15,540
then get into the blood, hit the breast, and then cause it to produce milk.

108
00:06:15,740 --> 00:06:17,300
That could result in galacteria.

109
00:06:18,020 --> 00:06:22,520
And finally, we can see galacteria as a consequence of a side effect of some drugs.

110
00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:27,320
And so these are the few important causes to be aware of concerning galacteria.

111
00:06:27,640 --> 00:06:30,840
And that closes off our introduction, and we're going to move to inflammatory conditions

112
00:06:30,840 --> 00:06:31,680
in the next section.

113
00:06:31,680 --> 00:07:01,660
So, we're going to move to an end of the next section.

